'''''Sòtol Community Coalition v. Tramuntana Provincial Government''''' (2016–2017) was a {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which the Sòtol Community Coalition, a network of LGBT organisations, challenged the [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] provincial government's allocation of social services funding and its education curriculum. The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled partially in favour of the Coalition, finding that the province's funding allocation patterns constituted indirect {{w|discrimination}} but that the curriculum matter required legislative rather than judicial resolution.

== Background ==

The [[Sòtol Community Coalition]] was a network of LGBT organisations founded by [[Miquel Esteve i Cardona]], based in the [[Sòtol, Sant Cristòfor|Sòtol]] neighbourhood of [[Sant Cristòfor]]. The Coalition's member organisations provided a range of services including counselling, health education, community outreach, and support for LGBT youth. While the Coalition was based in Sant Cristòfor, several member organisations operated outreach programmes in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Nurra Province|Nurra]].

In 2015, the Coalition published a report documenting that Tramuntana Province's social services funding systematically excluded LGBT community organisations. The report found that in the period 2010–2015, the Tramuntana provincial government had allocated no funding to any LGBT-focused organisation, despite receiving applications from several Coalition members. The report also documented that the province's secondary school curriculum included no mention of {{w|sexual orientation}} or {{w|gender identity}}, in contrast to the curricula of [[Estret Province|Estret]] and [[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]], which had voluntarily included such content.

== Case ==

The Coalition brought a case before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 2016, making two claims. First, the Coalition alleged that the provincial government's social services funding allocation constituted indirect discrimination on the basis of sexual orientation under the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] (2008). The Coalition argued that the funding criteria, while facially neutral, had a disparate impact on LGBT organisations and that the province's pattern of zero funding over five years could not be explained by neutral factors.

Second, the Coalition alleged that the absence of any mention of sexual orientation or gender identity in the Tramuntana education curriculum constituted discrimination in education under the Twelve Guarantees. The Coalition argued that the complete erasure of LGBT topics from the curriculum denied LGBT students access to relevant health and safety information and reinforced stigma.

The Tramuntana provincial government defended its funding decisions on the grounds that its social services budget was limited and that priority was given to organisations serving the broadest segments of the population. The province argued that LGBT organisations, which served a smaller population, were not excluded on the basis of sexual orientation but on the basis of population reach. On the curriculum issue, the province argued that education content was a matter for the provincial government's discretion and that the absence of LGBT content did not constitute discrimination, as the curriculum did not address sexual orientation in any form.

== Ruling ==

The Tribunal ruled partially in favour of the Coalition in 2017.

On the funding issue, the Tribunal found that the provincial government's funding allocation patterns constituted indirect discrimination. The Tribunal held that the complete absence of funding for LGBT organisations over a five-year period, despite the receipt of applications, could not be explained by neutral factors alone. The Tribunal ordered Tramuntana Province to revise its social services funding criteria to include LGBT organisations and to report compliance to the Tribunal annually.

On the curriculum issue, the Tribunal held that the matter required legislative rather than judicial resolution. The Tribunal noted that the Twelve Guarantees prohibited discrimination in education but did not mandate specific curriculum content, and that the question of whether the absence of LGBT content constituted discrimination was a matter for the National Assembly to address through legislation.

== Significance ==

The Sòtol Community Coalition case was significant for establishing that indirect discrimination — disparate impact resulting from facially neutral policies — was actionable under the Twelve Guarantees framework. The ruling required Tramuntana Province to revise its funding practices and established the principle that provincial governments could not exclude LGBT organisations from public funding on the basis of population reach alone.

The curriculum issue raised in the case was subsequently addressed by the 2019 Inclusive Education Amendment, which mandated that all provinces include age-appropriate content on sexual orientation and gender identity in secondary school curricula. The case was also cited in the campaign for the [[Constitutional Amendment on Gender Identity Protections]] (2019), as it demonstrated that the absence of constitutional protections for gender identity left transgender individuals vulnerable to discrimination in provincial services.

